The integrated equivalent form. For fixed $x$ put $w(z):=(1-|z|^2)^{-1/2}$ on $B_1\subset\mathbb R^2$ and $A(x,t):=\int_{B_1}u_0(x+ctz)w(z)\,dz$. By [F3], $w\in L^1(B_1)$. To differentiate in $t$, fix compact sets $K\subset\mathbb R^2$ and $J\subset(0,\infty)$ for $x$ and $t$. Choose a closed ball $Q$ containing $x+c(t+s)z$ for $x\in K$, $t\in J$, $|s|$ sufficiently small and $|z|\le1$. By [F6], $C:=\sup_Q|
abla u_0|<\infty$. The difference quotients of $u_0(x+ctz)$ in $t$, for these parameters and sufficiently small increments $h$, are bounded in absolute value by $cC|z|$ by [F7]. After multiplication by $w(z)$ they are dominated by the locally uniform integrable function $cC|z|w(z)$. They converge pointwise to $c
abla u_0(x+ctz)\cdot z\,w(z)$, so [F4] gives $\partial_tA(x,t)=c\int_{B_1}
abla u_0(x+ctz)\cdot z\,w(z)\,dz$. The same argument for spatial difference quotients, and dominated convergence applied to convergent parameter sequences with the same compact-set majorant, shows that these first derivatives are continuous locally; in particular $A$ is $C^1$ in $(x,t)$. Now [F5] gives $I(x,t):=\int_{B_{ct}(x)}u_0(y)(c^2t^2-|y-x|^2)^{-1/2}dy=ctA(x,t)$, hence $\partial_t I=cA+ct\partial_tA$. The same change of variables gives $\int_{B_{ct}(x)}
abla u_0(y)\cdot(y-x)(c^2t^2-|y-x|^2)^{-1/2}dy=(ct)^2\int_{B_1}
abla u_0(x+ctz)\cdot z\,w(z)\,dz=(ct)^2\partial_tA/c$. Therefore $ rac{1}{2\pi c} igl[\partial_t I+\int_{B_{ct}(x)}u_1(y)(c^2t^2-|y-x|^2)^{-1/2}dy igr]= rac{1}{2\pi ct}\int_{B_{ct}(x)} rac{u_0(y)+
abla u_0(y)\cdot(y-x)+tu_1(y)}{\sqrt{c^2t^2-|y-x|^2}}dy$, which is the equivalent form.
